**1. Immutable class.** An object whose state cannot change after construction, so it is inherently thread-safe and safe as a map key. Rules: declare the class `final` (or make constructors private); make all fields `private final`; no setters; **defensively copy mutable inputs in the constructor and mutable outputs in getters**.

```
public final class Employee {
  private final int id;
  private final String name;                 // String is immutable
  private final Map<String,String> attrs;
  private final List<String> skills;
  private final Address address;             // must itself be immutable

  public Employee(int id, String name, Map<String,String> attrs,
                  List<String> skills, Address address) {
    this.id = id; this.name = name;
    this.attrs = Collections.unmodifiableMap(new HashMap<>(attrs));
    this.skills = List.copyOf(skills);
    this.address = address;
  }
  public Map<String,String> getAttrs(){ return attrs; }   // unmodifiable view
  public List<String> getSkills(){ return skills; }
}
```

The `HashMap` and `ArrayList` are the traps: storing the caller's reference lets them mutate your state later, and `final` only prevents reassigning the reference, not changing contents. The copy plus unmodifiable wrapper gives **deep** immutability only if the elements are immutable too (here Strings). If elements were mutable, copy each.

**2. Thread-safe singleton.** Prefer the **enum singleton** (`enum Registry { INSTANCE; }`), or the **initialization-on-demand holder**: a private static nested class holding the instance, which the JVM initialises lazily and safely. Double-checked locking also works, but only with a `volatile` field:

```
private static volatile Singleton inst;
static Singleton get(){ if(inst==null){ synchronized(Singleton.class){ if(inst==null) inst=new Singleton(); } } return inst; }
```

Without `volatile`, another thread may see a partially constructed object due to instruction reordering. Also make the constructor private and guard against reflection/serialization if required (enum handles both).

**3. Pass by value.** Java always copies the argument. For primitives, the value is copied. For objects, the **reference value** is copied, so the method can mutate the object the caller sees, but reassigning the parameter (`p = new Foo()`) does not affect the caller's variable. That is why a swap method for two objects fails.

**Avoid:** claiming `final` makes contents immutable, and unsafe lazy singletons without `volatile`.
